selftext:
First entry of a short devlog series for **The Old Circle**, a dark top-down ARPG I'm building solo with Claude Code as my main coding partner.

**What's in the video:** every one of the 21 dungeons now hides a secret room behind a crackable wall. Each room is one of four things:

* a puzzle;
* a mechanic that exists only in that room (one turns you into a soul for a few seconds);
* a mini-boss;
* an NPC who asks a favor;

and some are cursed pact rooms, where you take a power and pay a price.

**The AI part (the funny part):** when I asked for puzzles across all 21 acts, the first pass kept producing the same 7 puzzles with new names and new colors. So the rule became a test. Every room has a fingerprint of its rules and mechanics. If two rooms share one, the build fails. Each room also has a scripted solver, so the test checks that every puzzle can actually be solved. That's the red ✗ turning into a ✓ at the end of the video.

**How the video was made:** it's not screen-recorded. The game runs headless with a virtual clock and gets rendered frame by frame. The hero walks with grid pathfinding (the first version walked into walls, and people noticed). The puzzles are solved by the same solvers the tests use. The music is synthesized in code. Text only, no AI voice.

**Next episode:** what happens when you accept the wrong pact.

Happy to answer questions about the workflow: what I let the AI do alone, what I review line by line, and what broke.

